run#aCorrectiveRoundIsHandedTheCorrections| ## Where the project's rules are
run#aCorrectiveRoundIsHandedTheCorrections| 
run#aCorrectiveRoundIsHandedTheCorrections| Your system prompt carries **no project context**: this child is started without the project's context files, so
run#aCorrectiveRoundIsHandedTheCorrections| what `AGENTS.md` would have handed you on every turn is here instead, once. It is the same routing, and it is
run#aCorrectiveRoundIsHandedTheCorrections| the whole of it:
run#aCorrectiveRoundIsHandedTheCorrections| 
run#aCorrectiveRoundIsHandedTheCorrections| - `AGENTS.md`, in the project root, is the project's **document map**: it routes a kind of change to the guideline
run#aCorrectiveRoundIsHandedTheCorrections|   that owns the rule. Find your change's row there, then read the section it names — never the whole file.
run#aCorrectiveRoundIsHandedTheCorrections| - Every guideline is one document under `guidelines/`, and its section list is the document's own:
run#aCorrectiveRoundIsHandedTheCorrections|   `tools/look.sh '^## ' --path guidelines/<file>.md` prints it, so you read the section that owns the rule and never
run#aCorrectiveRoundIsHandedTheCorrections|   the whole document. That wrapper is what a tree question is answered with here: the shell form of it is refused by
run#aCorrectiveRoundIsHandedTheCorrections|   the policy every child is started with (`tools/pi-shell-refusal.mjs`, its verb list in the block below), and a tool
run#aCorrectiveRoundIsHandedTheCorrections|   that fails on your host is not an answer about the tree either — the wrapper is a call, not a hint.
run#aCorrectiveRoundIsHandedTheCorrections| - The shipped capabilities are under `openspec/specs/`, one `spec.md` per capability; the change you plan lives in
run#aCorrectiveRoundIsHandedTheCorrections|   `openspec/changes/<change-name>/`, and the evidence it cites is versioned in that change's `evidence/`.
run#aCorrectiveRoundIsHandedTheCorrections| - The rules a phase like yours breaks most often are the ones measuring them: what the gate's result means and
run#aCorrectiveRoundIsHandedTheCorrections|   what a red run is, the test constraints, and the rule that **no log or diagnostics line carries a coordinate** —
run#aCorrectiveRoundIsHandedTheCorrections|   an object, a database or file name, a magnification, a pixel, an accuracy or a bearing instead.
run#aCorrectiveRoundIsHandedTheCorrections| 
run#aCorrectiveRoundIsHandedTheCorrections| ## What your own shell and tools refuse
run#aCorrectiveRoundIsHandedTheCorrections| 
run#aCorrectiveRoundIsHandedTheCorrections| None of the four families below is a question you can ask and none of them is a rule of this project to be argued
run#aCorrectiveRoundIsHandedTheCorrections| with: each is what the process you are started in refuses, before any rule of this tree can matter (issue #799).
run#aCorrectiveRoundIsHandedTheCorrections| 
run#aCorrectiveRoundIsHandedTheCorrections| - **You commit nothing and stage nothing.** The run commits, and the branch the item descends from is the base a
run#aCorrectiveRoundIsHandedTheCorrections|   red-on-HEAD proof is taken against, so `git commit` and `git add` are no step of yours — and `git stash`,
run#aCorrectiveRoundIsHandedTheCorrections|   `git checkout`, `git apply` and `git worktree` come back refused, because the approval they need is one an
run#aCorrectiveRoundIsHandedTheCorrections|   unattended process can never be asked for.
run#aCorrectiveRoundIsHandedTheCorrections| - **No inline code.** `python3 -c`, `python3 <<'EOF'` and a `$( … )` substitution are refused. A number is
run#aCorrectiveRoundIsHandedTheCorrections|   taken by a command the tree already carries — the project's measuring command, the suite a change names, a
run#aCorrectiveRoundIsHandedTheCorrections|   `tools/*.sh` — or by `awk` over the output such a command printed.
run#aCorrectiveRoundIsHandedTheCorrections| - **An edit needs the text as it stands now.** A body read twenty turns ago comes back `could not find the exact
run#aCorrectiveRoundIsHandedTheCorrections|   text`, or `found N occurrences` when the text is not unique: read the range once with `ctx_read` immediately before
run#aCorrectiveRoundIsHandedTheCorrections|   the edit, or edit with `ctx_patch`, whose hash refuses with a conflict instead of writing the wrong text.
run#aCorrectiveRoundIsHandedTheCorrections| - **A shell search of the tree is refused**: the verbs `grep`, `cat`, `head`, `tail`, `ls`, `find`, `wc` and `awk`
run#aCorrectiveRoundIsHandedTheCorrections|   over the tree are `tools/look.sh` (a pattern over the whole tree), `ctx_read` (one file, or a range of it) and
run#aCorrectiveRoundIsHandedTheCorrections|   `ctx_ls` (a directory). A range read (`sed -n 'N,Mp'`, `tail -n`) and a filter of another command's output are not
run#aCorrectiveRoundIsHandedTheCorrections|   refused.
run#aCorrectiveRoundIsHandedTheCorrections| 
run#aCorrectiveRoundIsHandedTheCorrections| ## One call per measurement, and one turn for the independent ones
run#aCorrectiveRoundIsHandedTheCorrections| 
run#aCorrectiveRoundIsHandedTheCorrections| Run `./tools/loop/measure.sh` once, from the change root, before you plan. It prints the change's own numbers as one
run#aCorrectiveRoundIsHandedTheCorrections| block — the paths it touches, the checks that pathspec owes (with the command that runs each), the tallies the
run#aCorrectiveRoundIsHandedTheCorrections| tree already carries, the project's declared case count with the command that printed it, the gate's own
run#aCorrectiveRoundIsHandedTheCorrections| measured timing, and the change's spec scenarios counted against the cases that name them. **It runs none of
run#aCorrectiveRoundIsHandedTheCorrections| them**: the gate is run once, by `./tools/loop/gate.sh`, and a number the block lacks is a defect in the block rather
run#aCorrectiveRoundIsHandedTheCorrections| than a reason to walk the tree by hand.
run#aCorrectiveRoundIsHandedTheCorrections| 
run#aCorrectiveRoundIsHandedTheCorrections| **Which selection is whose.** The `LOOP-MEASURE-CHECK` lines are the gate's: the gate owns the full selection
run#aCorrectiveRoundIsHandedTheCorrections| its own step names — the child that runs it once, or your own last phase when the run makes the gate yours — and a
run#aCorrectiveRoundIsHandedTheCorrections| check you run to prove one of them is that selection paid twice. Yours is the named red case and the cases your
run#aCorrectiveRoundIsHandedTheCorrections| delta reaches: run those while you iterate, and cite the block for every other check instead of running it. Two
