## Phase A — root cause and the red test

1. Verify the entry against the tree. Confirm the condition still holds, with a `file:line`, and that it
   is not already fixed. If it no longer holds, stop: verdict `skipped`, sub-verdict `stale`, with the
   `file:line` that shows the claim is gone. A stale claim carries no measurement and no review, so the run
   does not retire the item on it: it escalates it, and your `LOOP-DECISION` is the question a person answers —
   retire it as stale, or re-diagnose it. When the `ITEM_CHANGE` block names a change, this step verifies
   *that* change's claims first: a cited `file:line` that moved, or a red case that is green on the base, is
   `needs-diagnosis` naming the path, not an invitation to plan again.
2. Name the root cause in one sentence: the single mechanism that explains the symptom. Two mechanisms
   means the item is not eligible — verdict `skipped`, sub-verdict `needs-diagnosis`. For `feature`
   there is no root cause to name: name the one missing capability and the `file:line` (or the named run)
   that shows it is absent, and treat two independent missing capabilities as `needs-diagnosis`.
3. Produce the **red test**: the smallest case that exercises the defect and fails on HEAD. Record the
   JUnit XML `tests=`/`failures=`, the case name and the timestamp. For a non-defect, the red case
   asserts the number, count or absence the delta requires — never "better". For `feature`, the
   red case is the acceptance scenario the new requirement states: it fails on HEAD because the capability is
   absent, and it passes only once the capability exists.
4. Name the owning capability and the owning guideline section. Route with `AGENTS.md`'s Documentation
   Map; never read a guideline in full.
5. Count the surface before the plan is fixed. The rule you are about to change has more than one place that
   asserts it, and the plan is only real once you know how many: `tools/look.sh '<the rule>'` names the files that
   hold the rule's text and its symbol — over the cases (`tools/*-cases.sh`), the engine, the prompts and the
   fixtures — and lists the cases and fixtures that assert the behaviour being changed. The proposal carries the count with the command that produced it
   (`surface: 2 cases added, 2 rewritten, 4 fixtures realigned — <command>`), because the count, not the first
   file the plan names, is what the estimate is set against. An estimate made before this pass is the defect
   this step exists for: the #232 change was planned as two functions and landed as 292 insertions over six
   files (issue #238).

## Phase B — the eligibility gate (strict)

Decide, and on a rejection do not ask: record the verdict and the one clause of evidence, and stop. The
run's pre-screen already decided what a script can decide; these are the judgement gates it left to you.

| # | condition | if it fails |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | the claim is verified in the tree today | `stale` |
| 2 | one root cause explains the symptom, or for `feature` one missing capability explains the item | `needs-diagnosis` |
| 3 | exactly one change follows — no trade-off, no option, no design fork, no reversal of a recorded owner decision | `needs-decision` |
| 4 | it is a falsifiable WHEN/THEN that is false on HEAD — for `feature`, the acceptance scenario fails because the capability is absent | `unspecifiable` |
| 5 | it fits one capability delta and one change (bounded files) | `too-wide` |
| 6 | correctness is decidable by a host test | `needs-device` |
| 7 | *(a non-defect)* an existing capability owns the delta — or `feature` adds one small requirement — and the red case asserts a number, count, absence or acceptance scenario, not "better" | `not-spec-shaped` |

Never renumber, never re-rank and never delete on a rejection: reporting the verdicts **is** part of this
run's output.

## Phase C — plan the change

When the `ITEM_CHANGE` block names a change, there is nothing to plan: apply that change's `tasks.md`, in order, and
write no change directory of your own. The rest of this section is for an item that names none.

Use the planning skill from the list above; it must not touch project code. Change name: `fix-<slug>`, or
`add-<slug>` when the class is `feature`. A propose-class item's plan is written **before** any
production code: proposal, capability delta, design and tasks, then apply — the propose-first path.

- `proposal.md`'s `## Why` carries the Phase A evidence verbatim (root cause, the red test and its XML,
  the owning requirement, the guideline section; for `feature`: the absence with its pointer and
  the acceptance scenario).
- `specs/<capability>/spec.md` — the delta only, and every scenario names exactly one test case. A
  scenario with no case is a planning defect: fix the plan rather than promising a test later.
- `design.md` — the root cause, the single fix, blast radius, rollback, and the alternatives the evidence
  **eliminated**. A list of viable options means gate 3 was wrong: stop and re-gate.
- `tasks.md` — ordered, each with its own verification. A change that touches no compiled file replaces
  its build task with the structural check of the file it edits and says so instead of running a build
  "for completeness".

## Phase D — apply

Use the apply and verification skills from the list above. Iterate with focused suites.

`./tools/loop/measure.sh` is not the gate and does not stand in for it: it reads, the gate runs. Run it again only when
the pathspec or the change has moved, and quote the block you already have when nothing has.

Scoped by what the change touched: no compiled file — no gate, the structural check stands in and you say
so; test sources only — the red case plus the affected cases; code — the gate. Do not re-run a gate
to "refresh" evidence: the re-run is what destroys it, so copy the XMLs you cite into `/home/tim/projects/NaviVeylin/.pi/tmp/loop-selftest.PxHy1l/cases/5/tree/case.xAmtjb/repo/.pi/issue-loop/items/21`
at the moment they are produced. Never mark a task done on a cached or partial run, and never while a
failure is open. Tests red after three focused attempts is `blocked`, not "adjust the test".

**The plan's first line is the cheapest check a delta can owe, and it goes first.** A diff that writes a case, or
narrows the first-line `# files:` declaration that says what a case can reach, is refused by one case in seconds
rather than by minutes of suite, and the gate's plan already puts that step first for such a delta. Ask for the
plan (`./tools/loop/gate.sh --plan --base b840dab6f6510cf23a27c3be058dd86660f7ded3`) and run its first line before you run anything else: the suite that
would otherwise refuse the declaration arrives at the end of the gate, and the attempt that paid for it was
recorded `blocked` with nothing archived (issue #792). Do not write a second reading of the declaration — the one
case that reads it stays its only owner.

## Grade your own diff before you hand it over

The reader who follows you refuses a diff on three predicates, and each one is answerable from the diff in your
hands — `openspec/specs/agent-issue-loop/spec.md`, "A review finding is blocking or it is not". Read your own
diff against them before you print the line that ends this session, and answer what you find instead of handing
it over:

1. **Every scenario's clauses against the case that names it.** For each `- **THEN**` clause of your delta, point at
   the line of the case that fails once the production change is removed. A case whose name matches and whose
   assertions cover the first clause only is the finding a reader writes next.
2. **Your own prose against the diff.** Every sentence of `design.md`, `tasks.md` and `proposal.md` that claims
   something about the tree — "no guideline section states it", "keeps today's text byte for byte", a
   `path:line` pointer — is either made true by a hunk you can name or deleted. The claim you cannot point at is
   the one a reader cannot check without you.
3. **Every artifact your change cites.** A log, XML, evidence file or case name your change or your report names
   exists where it says, now. Copy it into `/home/tim/projects/NaviVeylin/.pi/tmp/loop-selftest.PxHy1l/cases/5/tree/case.xAmtjb/repo/.pi/issue-loop/items/21` when it is produced, never after.
4. **The owning guideline section phase C named.** It carries the edit this diff owes, or the change states why it
   owes none — and a `design.md` that asserts no section owns the rule is a claim this item proves or withdraws.

A finding you make here is not a reason to stop. Fix it, or — when it is outside this item — name it as a `NOTE:`
in your report, so the reviewer meets it already named rather than paying a round to find it.

**Complete the change before you run the gate** (issue #837). The gate names the tree it measures and a round's diff
names the tree it was written from, and those two names have to be one — so everything that writes the change
directory is done *before* the gate: copy the XMLs you cite into the change's own `evidence/` directory and into
`/home/tim/projects/NaviVeylin/.pi/tmp/loop-selftest.PxHy1l/cases/5/tree/case.xAmtjb/repo/.pi/issue-loop/items/21`, run `bash tools/loop/evidence-check.sh <the change directory> --root .` from the change root and
fix what it reports, and archive the change with the delta it merges (`openspec archive --yes`) — the change child is
this child in this shape, so the archive is yours; a rework round starts with the change already archived, and there
you complete nothing and leave the directory where it is. The date prefix the
archive adds is what the profile's `LOOP_CHANGE_ARCHIVE_PREFIX` strips from a path before it is hashed, so the tree
reads the same before and after the move. Nothing outside the change's `evidence/` directory is written after the gate:
a repair made there is a tree the gate never measured.

Run the project's gate **once**, exactly as the run's configuration states it:

```
cd /home/tim/projects/NaviVeylin/.pi/tmp/loop-selftest.PxHy1l/cases/5/tree/case.xAmtjb/repo/.pi/worktrees/loop {{GATE_STEP}}{{GATE_STEP}} nohup ./tools/loop/gate.sh > /home/tim/projects/NaviVeylin/.pi/tmp/loop-selftest.PxHy1l/cases/5/tree/case.xAmtjb/repo/.pi/issue-loop/items/21/gate.log 2>{{GATE_STEP}}1 {{GATE_STEP}}
```

A shell whose policy refuses to let one program start another drops the `nohup` and keeps the trailing `{{GATE_STEP}}` with both
streams in that log; the gate outlives the call either way. Keep the path absolute as it stands — `/home/tim/projects/NaviVeylin/.pi/tmp/loop-selftest.PxHy1l/cases/5/tree/case.xAmtjb/repo/.pi/issue-loop/items/21`
is, and a bare `gate.log` would land in the project's own tree.

**The wall this call runs into, and the way through it (issue #957).** The harness you run under ends one tool call
at a **ceiling** of its own — 900 s measured on this host, 1 500 s in the run that filed this issue — and the gate is
bounded by the profile's own clocks, which are longer than that (`LOOP_GATE_STEP_MAX_SECONDS` for one step,
`LOOP_ITEM_GATE_SECONDS` for an item's whole gate). So a gate that passes can outlast the call that started it, and a
gate run inline ends as a call that was cut: no `LOOP-GATE:` line, no tally, and a diff the round after the gate
refuses for carrying no gate of its own. So the gate is **started detached and polled** — the call that starts it is
not the call that reads it — and each poll is a wait and a range read, which your shell allows, while `grep`, `cat` and
`head` over that log are the tree search it refuses:

```
sleep 600 {{GATE_STEP}}{{GATE_STEP}} tail -n 40 /home/tim/projects/NaviVeylin/.pi/tmp/loop-selftest.PxHy1l/cases/5/tree/case.xAmtjb/repo/.pi/issue-loop/items/21/gate.log
```

The gate is over when the log's last line begins `LOOP-GATE:` — its verdict, the numbers your report quotes — or
`LOOP-GATE-REFUSED:`, which measured nothing. **A call that ends at its ceiling is the call ending, not the gate**: the
process it started keeps running (this loop's own record, `tools/loop/loop.conf`), so resume by polling again, and
never start a second gate — a gate started while one is running refuses with `LOOP-GATE-REFUSED: another gate of this
tree holds …` and exit `4`, which is the engine saying one gate per tree and not a red gate to repair. Do not end your
session before that log carries the tally: the gate line you print below is the gate's own, copied.

The gate is evidence, not a thing to retry: it is run once per item and its result is quoted, not improved. A
gate that does not complete inside the run's budget — the run ends a child that outlasts its configured
wall-clock, and that includes every command you started — is not yours to report: the run records the item
`blocked` with your log itself, and a second attempt is not yours to make. Two attempts at one gate is how a run
spends its whole budget inside one child.

**The gate is where your session ends.** When it has run, print the gate block below and stop: copy the XMLs you
cite into `/home/tim/projects/NaviVeylin/.pi/tmp/loop-selftest.PxHy1l/cases/5/tree/case.xAmtjb/repo/.pi/issue-loop/items/21` as they are produced, and write nothing else into the tree. The run starts the tail process
from those lines, and that process reads the archived change's `tasks.md`, the gate line and the last 200 lines of your
own tool output — which is why you stop here rather than carrying this session's whole context through the record.

A gate that did not pass is not yours to repair either: the run restores the change to its unarchived name and the tail
records the item `blocked` with the failing output you printed. What you must leave behind is the evidence of what you
